<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-2-1-2-sentences" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Basic Sentence Structure</title>


<p>
    Basic sentence structure for Louisiana Creole is SVO just like in English:
</p>


          
        <tabular>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell><em> subject + verb + object </em></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    


<p> </p>

<p></p>


<p>
    Subject is what we are talking about. It can be a noun or a pronoun. 
</p>

<p>
    We will discuss verbs and tenses in a later section. 
</p>

<p>
    Object is a noun or pronoun that receives or is the target of the subject. 
</p>


    <example>
   <p> Example: The ball rolls down the hill.
</p>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
    Subject: ball
</p>

<p>
    Verb: rolls
</p>

<p>
    Object: hill
</p>

<p>
    down is used as an adverb since it modifies the verb "rolls".
</p>
</solution>

<p>
    In LC the example sentence above would be: Laplòt roulé enba koto.
</p>

<p>
    A general rule for speaking in LC is to leave out any unnecessary information. 
    Many prepositions are left out of sentences if they can be implied. Notice we don't use a
    "the" term for the hill (koto) or ball (laplòt) since it adds no additional information to
     the sentence.  "The" would only be necessary if there were several hills or balls present and we needed to specify a 
    certain one or group.
</p>

<p>
    Post-nominal determinaers are little words or phrases we add to the end of a noun to indicate
    plural and specifics. Think about it as when we say "the", the term for "the" goes at the end.
    We can use the standard French form of "la" in front of a noun as well.
</p>

<example>
 <p>la shyin and shyin-la both mean "the dog". </p>
</example>

 <solution>
   <p> The first is a more acrolectal form (Fr) and the second
  is more basilectal (LC). </p>
  </solution>







  <!--begin notebookLM section w/ several sources-->
  <subsubsection xml:id="sub-svo-order">
    <title>Basic Word Order (SVO)</title>
    <p>
      Just like in English, Kouri-Vini follows a <b>Subject-Verb-Object (SVO)</b> word order [1, 3]. 
      This means you start with the person or thing doing the action, followed by the action itself, and then the person or thing receiving the action [1, 4].
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        English: "The man has a dog."
      </p>
      <p>
        Creole: <b>Nonm-la gin in shyin.</b> [1]
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Nonm</b> (Man) is the Subject [1].</li>
        <li><b>Gin</b> (To have) is the Verb [1, 4].</li>
        <li><b>Shyin</b> (Dog) is the Object [1].</li>
      </ul>
    </example>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="sub-determiners">
    <title>The Word for "The" (Post-Nominal Determiners)</title>
    <p>
      In English, we put the word "the" in front of a noun. In Kouri-Vini, we usually snap it onto the <b>back</b> of the word using a hyphen [5-7]. 
      These are called post-nominal determiners [8-10].
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <b>For one thing (Singular):</b> Use <b>-la</b> [6, 7].
          <br />Example: <i>gaçon-la</i> (the boy) [11].
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <b>For many things (Plural):</b> Use <b>-yé</b> [6, 12].
          <br />Example: <i>fiy-yé</i> (the girls) [11].
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <b>Note:</b> Some nouns are "sticky" and have old markers fused to the front of them, like <b>latab</b> (table) or <b>dizèf</b> (egg) [13-15]. 
      The word for "a" or "an" (<b>in</b> or <b>un</b>) still stays in the front [16, 17].
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="sub-negation">
    <title>Saying "No" (Negation)</title>
    <p>
      To make a sentence negative, you use the word <b>pa</b> [18-20]. 
      Where you put this word depends on the type of action:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <b>Action Verbs:</b> For most actions happening now, <b>pa</b> follows the verb [18, 19, 21].
          <br />Example: <i>Mo bwa pa diven.</i> (I don't drink wine) [18, 19].
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <b>Stative Verbs:</b> For verbs that describe a state or "having" something (like <i>gin</i> for have, or <i>konné</i> for know), <b>pa</b> goes <b>before</b> the verb [18, 19, 22, 23].
          <br />Example: <i>Mo pa gin shyin.</i> (I don't have a dog) [19].
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="sub-to-be">
    <title>The Verb "To Be"</title>
    <p>
      Kouri-Vini handles the verb "to be" differently than English. Sometimes you use a word for it, and sometimes you don't.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <b>Zero Copula:</b> When you are using an <b>adjective</b> to describe someone (like "He is sick"), you do not need a word for "is" or "am" [24-27].
          <br />Example: <b>Li malad.</b> (He [is] sick) [26-28].
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <b>Equating Nouns:</b> If you are saying one thing <i>is</i> another thing (like "He is a man"), you must use <b>çé</b> (or <i>se</i>) [24, 25, 29, 30].
          <br />Example: <b>Li çé in nonm.</b> (He is a man) [29, 30].
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <b>Questions:</b> When asking a question like "Where are you?", the verb "to be" changes to <b>yê</b> and moves to the end [31-33].
          <br />Example: <b>Éyou to yê?</b> (Where are you?) [33].
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsubsection>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Try to translate these simple sentences into English using what you learned:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Mô pær çé in mèt-lékol.</li>
        <li>Mo konné pa.</li>
        <li>Shyin-yé faim.</li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <ol>
        <li>My father is a teacher. [8, 31, 34]</li>
        <li>I don't know. [18, 35]</li>
        <li>The dogs are hungry. [15, 26]</li>
      </ol>
    </solution>
  </exercise>






    </subsection> 